I'll claim responsibility for launching this whole ball of wax
(I'm at the beginnning of the Reference list at least).  In seeing
a couple responses to my post, I felt motivated to say something
about them.

In article <LCL.93Nov30221619@csa.bu.edu>, lcl@csa.bu.edu (Lisa Caroline Lewis)
says:
>
>A voice booms out:
>"Thou hast pleased me greatly.  Shall I make thee the Hand of Elbereth?" y/n
>
>at which we could all scream out N!!  The answer (IMO) should be permanent -
>you won't get asked again.  Even if we got *nothing* for that prayer round,
>anything is better than that "blessing".  (Though it would be sweeter if,
>since your god is ostensibly pleased with you, he grants you some other
>kindness.)  If you've gotten far enough to become the Hand, you likely don't
>need its benefits.  *Far* more important to be able to pray.

This idea I like a great deal.  After all, when I prayed in the first
place, I was hoping to either 1) get a bunch of ghost loot uncursed,
2) be given some insight about knowing what tune to play, or
3) some lesser blessing, along with my holy water.  The Hand of
Elbereth really came along at a bad time for me.

In article <2dh736$jc1@vtserf.cc.vt.edu>, joeshaw@info1.cc.vt.edu (Joseph Shaw)
says:
>
>If you've gotten far enough to become the Hand, you already have a
>powerful character:  Allowing that said powerful character to continue
>to get lots of goodies and blessings from their god would make that
>character even more powerful.

Unfortunately, I'm not a very powerful character.  I only just
recently squeaked in to Level 12.  I might have been Level 11 when
I was so blessed.  My AC is still only -5; I don't have enough cash
to throw at any priest; most of what I have I got from ghosts and
it will be an eon till I can hope for any more holy water.

>Lots of people are saying that the later parts of the game are
>too easy already; removing this would make it even worse.

Actually, I don't think so.  For one thing, most of us cannot pray
in Gehennom.  Also, once you've killed the WoY at least once, your
prayer timeout blows up again.  Certainly by That point, you have
a very powerful character.  At this point, I'd claim to still be
relatively wimpy.

In article <CHCEEn.Jvz@unix.portal.com>, mec@shell.portal.com (Michael Edward
Chastain) says:
>
>How about this: when you become the Hand / Envoy / Soul-Stealer, then you
>get a BIG addition to your score (like, your score DOUBLES).  I'd prefer
>my score to reflect how well I served my God rather than how long I spent
>polypiling bags of gems.

This thing looks kind of attractive.  However, I'd like to request a
modification to the idea: Double the score you earned Since your
patronage.  This will at least distinguish those who tried to grab
their brownie points near the endgame from those who were faithful
from the very beginning.

In article <2dhp40$1fj@pith.uoregon.edu>, seth@cie (Master Nethacker) says:
>
>I agree with Furey here.  I think that the prayer timeout HAS to be
>longer for touger characters, and this is a decent implementation.

As I noted before, it already is longer for the tough guys, assuming
that the only people who can pray are those who (1) have not gone to
Gehenoom and (2) those on their way out.  The characters in the latter
group have met the WoY and will have a long timeout from that.
I really don't picture people running up and down the stairways
in Gehennom just to pray (maybe That's why we have so many mazes?  :) )

>Proviso, though:
>
>Perhaps the prayers of the Hand ought to carry more weight, fewer though
>they be?  As it is, once I've thrashed the Castle, I pray once more,
>and by the time I have burned the Valley to the ground, I'm pretty
>much ready to pray one last time.  Then, down to the Wiz, and by the
>time I return, one or two last prayers before the Endgame stuff.

Given that a prayer isn't a wish, there aren't a whole lot of new
things you could get:  a few more HP, a random spellbook, or healing
effects that you should have available by other means already.

>Seriously, though... I DO know how it'd go if one of my typical 'winning'
>characters could pray every few hundred turns *shudder*

Again, a prayer isn't a wish.  Okay, if you could pray frequently,
you might get lots of spellbooks.  As I pointed out, though, you could
remove tha 'hand of Elbereth' clause from the current implementation,
and still have the 'killed Wizard' there to slow the wishes down.

I suddenly recall that your prayer timeout is also related to your
character level.  A level 15 character can see a prayer timeout
5 times as long as a low-level.  This increased timeout for
more powerful characters is already built into the game.

I guess mu whole sob story boils down from serving my God a little
TOO faithfully.  I sacrificed three unicorns in a row when I was on
dungeon level 4.  I haven't yet gotten any deeper in the Mines than
the Minetown (i.e. I haven't raided the cache at the bottom).
Since I'm barely Level 12, I have yet to Begin my Quest.

Does it sound like I'm too powerful a character to still be able
to grab a prayer every few hundred turns?   Maybe I'll start a
new contest:  Wimpiest character to get cursed with the Hand of Elbereth.

